			<VULN>
				<STIG_DATA>
					<VULN_ATTRIBUTE>Vuln_Num</VULN_ATTRIBUTE>
					<ATTRIBUTE_DATA>V-220641</ATTRIBUTE_DATA>
				</STIG_DATA>
				<STIG_DATA>
					<VULN_ATTRIBUTE>Severity</VULN_ATTRIBUTE>
					<ATTRIBUTE_DATA>medium</ATTRIBUTE_DATA>
				</STIG_DATA>
				<STIG_DATA>
					<VULN_ATTRIBUTE>Group_Title</VULN_ATTRIBUTE>
					<ATTRIBUTE_DATA>SRG-NET-000512-L2S-000007</ATTRIBUTE_DATA>
				</STIG_DATA>
				<STIG_DATA>
					<VULN_ATTRIBUTE>Rule_ID</VULN_ATTRIBUTE>
					<ATTRIBUTE_DATA>SV-220641r991848_rule</ATTRIBUTE_DATA>
				</STIG_DATA>
				<STIG_DATA>
					<VULN_ATTRIBUTE>Rule_Ver</VULN_ATTRIBUTE>
					<ATTRIBUTE_DATA>CISC-L2-000210</ATTRIBUTE_DATA>
				</STIG_DATA>
				<STIG_DATA>
					<VULN_ATTRIBUTE>Rule_Title</VULN_ATTRIBUTE>
					<ATTRIBUTE_DATA>The Cisco switch must have all disabled switch ports assigned to an unused VLAN.</ATTRIBUTE_DATA>
				</STIG_DATA>
				<STIG_DATA>
					<VULN_ATTRIBUTE>Vuln_Discuss</VULN_ATTRIBUTE>
					<ATTRIBUTE_DATA>A disabled port that is assigned to a user or management VLAN may become enabled by accident or by an attacker and as a result may gain access to that VLAN as a member.</ATTRIBUTE_DATA>
				</STIG_DATA>
				<STIG_DATA>
					<VULN_ATTRIBUTE>IA_Controls</VULN_ATTRIBUTE>
					<ATTRIBUTE_DATA />
				</STIG_DATA>
				<STIG_DATA>
					<VULN_ATTRIBUTE>Check_Content</VULN_ATTRIBUTE>
					<ATTRIBUTE_DATA>Step 1: Review the switch configurations and examine all access switch ports. Each access switch port not in use should have membership to an inactive VLAN. 

interface GigabitEthernet0/0
 switchport access vlan 999
 shutdown
!
interface GigabitEthernet0/1
 switchport access vlan 999
 shutdown
…
…
…
interface GigabitEthernet0/9
 switchport access vlan 999
 shutdown

Step 2: Verify that traffic from the inactive VLAN is not allowed on any trunk links as shown in the example below:

interface GigabitEthernet1/1
 switchport trunk allowed vlan 1-998,1000-4094
 switchport trunk encapsulation dot1q
 switchport mode trunk

Note: Switch ports configured for 802.1x are exempt from this requirement.

If any access switch ports are not in use and not in an inactive VLAN, this is a finding.</ATTRIBUTE_DATA>
				</STIG_DATA>
				<STIG_DATA>
					<VULN_ATTRIBUTE>Fix_Text</VULN_ATTRIBUTE>
					<ATTRIBUTE_DATA>Assign all switch ports not in use to an inactive VLAN.

Step 1: Assign the disabled interfaces to an inactive VLAN:

SW3(config)#int range g0/0 – 9
SW3(config-if-range)# switchport access vlan 999

Step 2: Configure trunk links to not allow traffic from the inactive VLAN:

SW3(config)#int g1/1
SW3(config-if)#switchport trunk allowed vlan except 999</ATTRIBUTE_DATA>
				</STIG_DATA>
				<STIG_DATA>
					<VULN_ATTRIBUTE>False_Positives</VULN_ATTRIBUTE>
					<ATTRIBUTE_DATA />
				</STIG_DATA>
				<STIG_DATA>
					<VULN_ATTRIBUTE>False_Negatives</VULN_ATTRIBUTE>
					<ATTRIBUTE_DATA />
				</STIG_DATA>
				<STIG_DATA>
					<VULN_ATTRIBUTE>Documentable</VULN_ATTRIBUTE>
					<ATTRIBUTE_DATA>false</ATTRIBUTE_DATA>
				</STIG_DATA>
				<STIG_DATA>
